# Keep the WORKING TREE on LF, not just the repo.
#
# ⚠ WHY THIS EXISTS — found 2026-09-09, and it cost half an hour of chasing
# nine "failures" that were not failures.
#
# This machine has `core.autocrlf=true`, so every clone, checkout, rebase or
# merge rewrites the working tree to CRLF. The repo content is LF either way —
# git normalises on the way in — so `git diff` shows nothing and the commit is
# clean. But `tests/handlers.test.mjs` is largely STATIC tests: they readFileSync
# airtable.js / index.html and assert against the source text, and several
# patterns match a literal `\n`. Against a CRLF working copy those see `\r\n`
# and fail.
#
# Nine tests went red at once immediately after a rebase, with no code change
# behind any of them. The failures name real invariants ("the reconcile writes
# NOTHING", "dismiss carries the guard in its WHERE clause"), so they read as
# genuine regressions — which is exactly the wrong thing for the repo's ONLY
# automated check to do. A suite that cries wolf after every checkout is a suite
# people learn to skip.
#
# `eol=lf` pins the working tree regardless of anyone's autocrlf setting, so the
# text the tests scan is the text that was committed.
#
# The alternative — rewriting every assertion to be line-ending agnostic — fixes
# today's nine and leaves the next one to be written as a trap.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary-ish things that must never be touched.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.pdf binary
*.ico binary
